# G075 — Bandwidth Monitor

*v2. See `bandwidth-monitor.md` for prose: time series as primary,
useful signal is the derivative not the counter, average smooths vs
peak preserves, counter wraparound as adversary not error, ring
buffer as bounded storage.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Per-Agent Throughput Monitoring

[monitor-agents
    IN ORDER TO
        sample every agent's message counter on a fixed cadence
        derive average and peak rates across rolling windows
        and alert when peak crosses a per-agent threshold

    @monitors <- [agents[all]] mapped to
        {agent: @a,
         monitor: [bw[new-monitor {capacity: 300}]]}

    every 1 second
        for @m in @monitors
            @count <- [agent[message-count {agent: @m:agent}]]
            [bw[record
                 {monitor: @m:monitor,
                  ts: [clock[now]],
                  bytes-total: @count}]]

            @avg  <- [bw[average-rate
                           {monitor: @m:monitor, window-ms: 60000}]]
            @peak <- [bw[peak-rate
                           {monitor: @m:monitor, window-ms: 300000}]]

            @peak
                where greater than @m:agent:peak-threshold
                    -> [alert[dispatch
                                {agent: @m:agent,
                                 avg: @avg, peak: @peak}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[bw
    new-monitor          {capacity}
    record               {monitor, ts, bytes-total}
    average-rate         {monitor, window-ms}
    peak-rate            {monitor, window-ms}
    samples              {monitor}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: rate as `where` derivative over window

[bw[average-rate {monitor: @m, window-ms: @w}]
    IN ORDER TO
        return bytes-per-second averaged over the last @w milliseconds
        from the monotonic counter stream @m is recording

    samples of @m
        where ts is within (now - @w) and now
        pair first and last such samples as (@a, @b)
        where @b:bytes-total is at least @a:bytes-total (no wrap)
            -> (@b:bytes-total - @a:bytes-total) × 1000
               / (@b:ts - @a:ts)
        otherwise
            -> skip this interval (wraparound; cannot measure)
               -> resume with next consecutive-increasing pair
]

The counter is the source of truth; every rate is a projection on
query. Same discipline every metrics system (Prometheus, StatsD,
the kernel's /proc/net/dev) encodes.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: average and peak as different `where` reducers

[bw[peak-rate {monitor: @m, window-ms: @w}]
    pairs of consecutive samples in the window
        reduced by maximum rate (not mean)
    IN ORDER TO
        preserve the spike that an averaged rate would smooth away
        so a 10x burst visible in peak stays visible
]

Average smooths; peak preserves. Both statistics on the same counter
data — pick the algorithm to match the question.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @m <- [bw[new-monitor {capacity: 100}]]
    [bw[record {monitor: @m, ts: 1000, bytes-total: 0}]]
    [bw[record {monitor: @m, ts: 2000, bytes-total: 1000}]]    ;; +1 KB/s
    [bw[record {monitor: @m, ts: 3000, bytes-total: 51000}]]   ;; +50 KB/s (spike)
    [bw[record {monitor: @m, ts: 4000, bytes-total: 52000}]]   ;; +1 KB/s

    -> [bw[average-rate {monitor: @m, window-ms: 3000}]]
    ;; -> ~17333 bytes/sec (spike smoothed into the mean)

    -> [bw[peak-rate {monitor: @m, window-ms: 3000}]]
    ;; -> 50000 bytes/sec (spike preserved)
]
